ocedure\": [\n        \"Calculate the covariance matrix of asset returns.\",\n        \"Compute eigenvalues of the covariance matrix.\",\n        \"Take the ratio of the largest eigenvalue to the sum of all eigenvalues.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 60},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 60,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude assets with missing data in the window; if fewer than 3 assets remain, return 0.0.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[0, 1]\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\", \"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"import numpy as np\\ncov_matrix = np.cov(returns_matrix)\\neigenvalues = np.linalg.eigvalsh(cov_matrix)\\nfeature_value = eigenvalues[0] / eigenvalues.sum()\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 0.0 when fewer than 3 assets in window\", \"Returns 1.0 for single-asset portfolios\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"downside_tail_comovement\",\n      \"name\": \"Downside Tail Co-Movement Index\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"MDD\", \"SOR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"Strong downside co-movement increases maximum drawdown risk, affecting Sortino-based algorithms more than Sharpe-based ones.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Let $r_t^i$ be asset $i$'s return at time $t$. Compute the average pairwise correlation on days where $\\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_i r_t^i < 0$.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Compute daily returns for each asset.\",\n        \"Identify days where the average return is negative.\",\n        \"Calculate pairwise correlations on those days and average them.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 20},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 10,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude days with fewer than 50% assets observed; return 0.0 if fewer than 3 downside days exist.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[-1, 1]\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\", \"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"import pandas as pd\\ndownside_days = returns.mean(axis=1) < 0\\nsubset_returns = returns.loc[downside_days]\\ncorrelations = subset_returns.corr().values\\nfeature_value = correlations[correlations != 1].mean()\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 0.0 when no downside days\", \"Returns 1.0 for perfectly correlated assets\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"nonstationary_correlation_variance\",\n      \"name\": \"Temporal Nonstationarity in Correlation\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"SHR\", \"SOR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"High variance in rolling correlations indicates nonstationarity, challenging covariance-dependent algorithms like mSSRM.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Compute rolling $w$-day average pairwise correlation, then calculate the variance of these values over time.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate daily pairwise correlations.\",\n        \"Apply a rolling $w$-day average to correlations.\",\n        \"Compute the variance of these rolling averages.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 20},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 30,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude days with missing assets; return 0.0 if fewer than 10 rolling windows exist.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[0, ∞)\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\", \"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"import pandas as pd\\nrolling_corr = returns.rolling(20).corr()\\nrolling_avg = rolling_corr.rolling(20).mean()\\nfeature_value = rolling_avg.var().mean()\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 0.0 for constant correlations\", \"Returns high value for rapidly changing correlations\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"momentum_disagreement_ratio\",\n      \"name\": \"Momentum Disagreement Index\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"AR\", \"CR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"High disagreement between short/long-term momentum signals may favor sequence models (LSTM/Mamba) over static algorithms.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Let $m_s^i$ and $m_l^i$ be short/long-term momentum for asset $i$. Compute $\\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_i |m_s^i - m_l^i|$.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate 5-day and 20-day momentum for each asset.\",\n        \"Compute absolute differences between short/long-term momentum.\",\n        \"Average across all assets.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"short_window\": 5, \"long_window\": 20},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 25,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude assets with missing momentum values; return 0.0 if fewer than 3 assets remain.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[0, ∞)\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\", \"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"short_mom = returns.rolling(5).mean()\\nlong_mom = returns.rolling(20).mean()\\nfeature_value = (abs(short_mom - long_mom).mean(axis=1).mean())\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 0.0 for perfectly aligned momentum\", \"Returns high value for divergent momentum signals\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"volatility_clustering_autocorr\",\n      \"name\": \"Volatility Clustering Autocorrelation\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"MDD\", \"SHR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"High autocorrelation of squared returns indicates volatility clustering, affecting risk estimation in EW/MCW algorithms.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Let $v_t = (r_t - \\\\bar{r})^2$. Compute the lag-1 autocorrelation of $v_t$.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate squared returns deviations from the mean.\",\n        \"Compute autocorrelation at lag 1 using the entire time series.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 60},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 60,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Linearly interpolate missing returns; return 0.0 if fewer than 40 observations exist.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[-1, 1]\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"import statsmodels.api as sm\\nsquared_returns = (returns - returns.mean())**2\\nfeature_value = sm.tsa.acf(squared_returns, nlags=1)[1]\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 0.0 for white noise\", \"Returns high value for clustered volatility\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"volume_price_synchronization\",\n      \"name\": \"Volume-Price Synchronization Index\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"MDD\", \"SHR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\", \"volume\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"Strong volume-price correlation suggests liquidity-driven price movements, potentially impacting MCW/EW algorithms.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Compute the Pearson correlation between daily absolute returns and volume.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate daily absolute returns: $|r_t|$.\",\n        \"Compute Pearson correlation with volume.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 60},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 60,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude days with missing volume; return 0.0 if fewer than 30 observations exist.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[-1, 1]\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"abs_returns = abs(returns)\\nfeature_value = abs_returns.corr(volume)\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 1.0 for perfectly synchronized volume/returns\", \"Returns 0.0 for uncorrelated data\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"covariance_condition_number\",\n      \"name\": \"Covariance Matrix Condition Number\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"SHR\", \"SOR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"High condition number indicates ill-conditioned covariance matrices, degrading performance of algorithms relying on precise estimation (e.g., mSSRM).\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Compute the ratio of largest to smallest eigenvalue of the covariance matrix.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate the covariance matrix of returns.\",\n        \"Compute eigenvalues and take the ratio of largest to smallest.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 60},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 60,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude assets with missing data; return 0.0 if fewer than 3 assets remain.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[1, ∞)\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\", \"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"eigenvalues = np.sort(np.linalg.eigvalsh(cov_matrix))[::-1]\\nfeature_value = eigenvalues[0] / eigenvalues[-1]\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 1.0 for identity covariance matrix\", \"Returns large value for ill-conditioned matrices\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"sample_size_to_dimensionality\",\n      \"name\": \"Sample-Size-to-Dimensionality Ratio\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"SHR\", \"SOR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"Low ratio (more assets than time points) stresses covariance estimation, impacting mSSRM and sequence models.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Let $T$ be the number of time points and $N$ the number of assets. Compute $\\\\frac{T}{N}$.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Count the number of time points $T$.\",\n        \"Count the number of assets $N$.\",\n        \"Compute $T/N$.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 60},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 60,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude assets with missing data; return 0.0 if $N > T$.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"(0, ∞)\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"T = len(returns)\\nN = returns.shape[1]\\nfeature_value = T / N\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 0.0 when assets exceed time points\", \"Returns high value for long time horizons\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"effective_rank_covariance\",\n      \"name\": \"Effective Rank of Covariance Matrix\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"SHR\", \"SOR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"Higher effective rank indicates more independent assets, challenging sparsity-based algorithms like mSSRM.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Let $\\\\lambda_i$ be eigenvalues of the covariance matrix. Compute $\\\\frac{(\\\\sum \\\\lambda_i)^2}{\\\\sum \\\\lambda_i^2}$.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate covariance matrix eigenvalues.\",\n        \"Apply the effective rank formula.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 60},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 60,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude assets with missing data; return 0.0 if fewer than 3 assets remain.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[1, N]\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\", \"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"eigenvalues = np.linalg.eigvalsh(cov_matrix)\\nfeature_value = (eigenvalues.sum()**2) / (eigenvalues**2).sum()\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 1.0 for single-asset portfolios\", \"Returns N for fully uncorrelated assets\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"downside_beta_concentration\",\n      \"name\": \"Downside Beta Concentration Index\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"MDD\", \"SOR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"Concentrated downside beta in few assets increases tail risk, affecting Sortino-based algorithms more.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Compute the Herfindahl index of downside betas (betas calculated using negative returns).\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate downside returns (negative returns only).\",\n        \"Estimate beta for each asset using linear regression on downside returns.\",\n        \"Compute Herfindahl index: $\\\\sum \\\\beta_i^2$.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 60},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 60,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude assets with missing downside returns; return 0.0 if fewer than 3 assets remain.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[0, 1]\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\", \"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"import statsmodels.api as sm\\ndownside_returns = returns[returns < 0]\\nX = sm.add_constant(downside_returns)\\nbetas = sm.OLS(downside_returns, X).fit().params\\nfeature_value = (betas**2).sum()\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 1.0 for single-asset portfolios\", \"Returns low value for evenly distributed betas\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"nonstationary_volatility_variance\",\n      \"name\": \"Nonstationarity in Volatility Regimes\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"SHR\", \"SOR\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"High volatility regime changes challenge risk models in Sharpe/Sortino-based algorithms.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Compute the variance of rolling volatility estimates over time.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate rolling volatility (standard deviation) for each asset.\",\n        \"Compute the variance of these volatility estimates across time.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 20},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 30,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude days with missing data; return 0.0 if fewer than 10 windows exist.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"[0, ∞)\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"rolling_vol = returns.rolling(20).std()\\nfeature_value = rolling_vol.var().mean()\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 0.0 for constant volatility\", \"Returns high value for volatile shifts\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature_id\": \"cross_asset_return_skewness\",\n      \"name\": \"Cross-Asset Return Skewness\",\n      \"criterion_scope\": [\"AR\", \"MDD\"],\n      \"inputs\": [\"div_adj_close\"],\n      \"hypothesis\": \"Positive skewness indicates fat right tails, potentially benefiting algorithms with asymmetric risk modeling.\",\n      \"mathematical_definition\": \"Compute the average skewness of asset returns across all assets.\",\n      \"procedure\": [\n        \"Calculate skewness for each asset's return distribution.\",\n        \"Average the skewness values across all assets.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\"window\": 60},\n      \"minimum_observations\": 60,\n      \"missing_data_policy\": \"Exclude assets with missing data; return 0.0 if fewer than 3 assets remain.\",\n      \"expected_range\": \"(-∞, ∞)\",\n      \"edge_case_fallback\": 0.0,\n      \"required_invariances\": [\"asset_permutation\", \"price_scale\"],\n      \"reference_python_pseudocode\": \"import scipy.stats\\nskewness = returns.apply(scipy.stats.skew)\\nfeature_value = skewness.mean()\",\n      \"unit_test_expectations\": [\"Returns 0.0 for symmetric returns\", \"Returns high value for positively skewed assets\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"Okay, let's tackle this problem step by step. The user wants 12 portfolio-level features that can help distinguish between the nine algorithms. The features need to be deterministic, computed from the given inputs, and each should be a single scalar per instance. They also need to cover various mathematical aspects like covariance geometry, effective dimensionality, downside dependence, etc.\n\nFirst, I need to list out the available inputs: date, company, open, h